        <h2 id="what-is">1. What a Spin Wheel Generator Actually Is</h2>
        <p>
          Think of it as a digital stand-in for the wheels used on classic game
          shows, except you decide what goes on it. Type in names, dish ideas,
          dares, raffle tickets — whatever you're choosing between — and each
          entry becomes a colored wedge.
        </p>
        <p>
          It runs entirely in the browser. There's nothing to install and
          nothing to configure beyond your list of options, which is a big part
          of why the format has spread well beyond classrooms into offices,
          livestreams, and casual group chats.
        </p>
        <p>
          What keeps people coming back to the wheel format specifically —
          rather than, say, a random number spat out silently — is that everyone
          watching sees the exact same process unfold at the exact same time.
          That shared moment is what makes the result feel earned rather than
          arbitrary.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-it-works">2. What's Happening Behind the Spin</h2>
        <p>
          It's fair to wonder whether an on-screen wheel is really random, or
          whether it just puts on a good show. Here's the mechanical reality.
        </p>
        <h3>Where the number comes from</h3>
        <p>
          When you press spin, our engine does not rely on basic
          <code>Math.random()</code> calls. Instead, it requests cryptographic
          entropy from the browser's <strong>Web Cryptography API</strong>
          (<code>window.crypto.getRandomValues()</code>) paired with rejection
          sampling to eliminate modulo bias, then maps that unbiased integer to a
          target landing angle on the wheel.
        </p>
        <p>
          Unlike predictable formula-based generators, each draw pulls fresh
          operating-system hardware entropy and runs completely independent of the
          last—every active slice keeps its exact mathematical share unless you
          intentionally adjust slice weights beforehand.
        </p>

        <div class="info-grid">
          <div class="info-card">
            <div class="ic-icon">🎯</div>
            <h4>Even odds, by default</h4>
            <p>
              Every entry starts at equal weight (1x) — adding more names shrinks
              the slices uniformly, never tilting fairness.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="info-card">
            <div class="ic-icon">⚖️</div>
            <h4>Weighted slices with one click</h4>
            <p>
              Tap the <code>+</code> or <code>-</code> weight buttons (1x to 20x)
              beside any entry to adjust odds cleanly — or enable duplicates for raffle tickets.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="info-card">
            <div class="ic-icon">👀</div>
            <h4>A process people can watch</h4>
            <p>
              Nothing happens off-screen — the spin itself is the evidence that
              nobody tampered with it.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="info-card">
